EN10366 

We draw the attention of the House to the prolonged processing times affecting Skilled Work Regional (Provisional) Subclass 491 Subsequent Entrant visa applications. 

Many applicants have waited more than 18 months while separated from their spouses and families. This prolonged uncertainty is placing significant emotional, financial and practical pressure on affected families. Newly married couples are being prevented from starting and building their lives together, while families remain separated without clear information about when their applications will be decided. 

Primary Subclass 491 visa holders are living, working, paying taxes and contributing to regional Australian communities. However, extended separation from their families can affect their wellbeing, relationships, work performance and ability to establish stable, long-term lives in regional Australia. 

Timely family reunification supports the objectives of Australia's regional migration program by helping skilled migrants settle successfully, remain productive and develop lasting connections with regional communities. 

We therefore ask the House to urge the Australian Government to: 

Review and expedite the processing of Subclass 491 Subsequent Entrant visa applications; 

Allocate sufficient resources to address the existing backlog; 

Give priority consideration to applications involving prolonged family separation; 

Publish clearer and more reliable information about expected processing timeframes; and 

Provide meaningful status updates to applicants whose applications have remained undecided for extended periods. 

We respectfully request prompt action to reduce unnecessary family separation and support the successful settlement of skilled migrants in regional Australia. 

from 108 citizens (Petition No. EN10366)

EN10371 

Nicotine pouches such as Zyn offer a significantly less harmful alternative for adult smokers and vapers seeking to reduce or quit their use of combustible cigarettes and vaping products. Unlike traditional smoking, these tobacco-free, smokeless pouches eliminate combustion, tar, and inhalation of harmful chemicals into the lungs, delivering nicotine in a discreet and controlled manner. International evidence and comparative studies show that oral nicotine products can substantially lower exposure to toxicants compared to cigarettes, supporting their role as a harm reduction tool for consenting adults. 

The blanket ban introduced in July 2026 removes lawful access for adults, pushes users toward unregulated black-market sources, and fails to differentiate between responsible adult use and youth protection. We strongly support strict age limits (18+), advertising restrictions targeting minors, and rigorous TGA quality/safety standards. However, prohibition does not protect public health—it undermines informed adult choice and ignores the reality that many Australians use these products specifically to move away from smoking. We urge the Government to review the ban, reinstate regulated personal importation or approval pathways, and adopt a balanced, evidence-based policy that prioritises harm reduction while safeguarding young people. 

We therefore ask the House to We call on the Government to: 

Review and repeal the July 2026 restrictions on nicotine pouches under the Personal Importation Scheme and related rules; 

Reinstate regulated access pathways for adults 18+; 

Direct the TGA to establish a framework for evaluating and approving quality nicotine pouches with appropriate safeguards; and 

Prioritise evidence-based harm reduction for current nicotine users while protecting young people. 

