GRIEVANCE DEBATE 

The DEPUTY SPEAKER ( Ms Payne ): I propose the question: 

That grievances be noted. 

Australian Society 

Mr WILKIE (Clark) (12:30): Many people are aggrieved right now or are at least anxious about the state of things. This is manifesting itself in all sorts of ways including politically, where loyalty to the major parties is breaking down and where interest in minor parties and Independents continues to increase. This heightened unease in the community hasn't come about suddenly, nor can it be attributed to anything in particular. Instead, it has come about over time, as described ably at the macro level by Waleed Aly, who wrote in the Guardian last week: 

… September 11 and the "war on terror" placed security front and centre. The 2005 London Tube bombings raised the spectre of homegrown terrorism, triggering all sorts of social anxieties. Wars in Afghanistan and especially Iraq revealed the limits of western military power and undermined trust in governments that either imagined or invented weapons of mass destruction. Then came the urgent awareness of climate change, which variously evoked existential dread and threatened to phase out entire industries on which depend economies and communities. Soon followed the global financial crisis, which caused millions around the world to lose their homes and life savings and revealed the edifice of our global economy to be more a house of cards than a cathedral. Then came Islamic State, reviving the spectre that began the century in the crudest, most brutal form. Next, Brexit and the election of Donald Trump—both previously unthinkable, both smashing all manner of orthodoxies, thrilling or terrifying according to taste. 

The dust hadn't even settled when Covid-19 arrived, shuttering economies and lives, inflicting psychological torment, radically expanding state power. It drove us into isolation and on to our phones, where we argued over lockdowns and masks and vaccines—seeding growing suspicion of authority, whether political or medical. Social media became ever more present and polarising. Covid abated but inflation took off. Then Hamas slaughtered Israeli civilians on 7 October 2023, and Israel annihilated Gaza in response with relative impunity, yielding unfathomable human suffering that ricocheted through cyberspace and into visceral local antagonism in societies around the world. Trump was re-elected and launched a trade war on the world which he called "Liberation Day". Then, in Australia, came Bondi. And while all this was happening, artificial intelligence emerged exponentially, developing faster than we could comprehend and threatening everything from swallowing up jobs by the million to unleashing killer robots. 

No wonder people are rattled, somewhat aggrieved and unhappy, frankly. Because in the face of all this governments, state and federal, have been swamped and have often taken their eye off the ball of progress or in some cases have actively allowed backsliding. So that's very much at the macro level. 

Now let's get down into the weeds and think about the things that are making people anxious and even angry in order to help in some way to explain the changing political dynamic at the moment. First and foremost, I think, is the escalating housing crisis, which is undermining the ability of Australians to access one of the most fundamental needs they have: a place to call home. Heavens, I know of people in my own electorate, people with jobs, that are living in cars. We probably are all aware of the thousands of people that are the hidden homeless, who are couch surfing at a friend's place, in the spare room or even in the shed out the back. In Hobart, there are beggars on the streets—in a rich place, in a rich country. In Tasmania, again, there are thousands of people on the public housing waiting list. 

There's the prolonged cost-of-living crisis, which undermines other fundamental needs, including putting food on the table three times a day. Of course, this is being driven by inflation, low real wage growth and cartel-like behaviour from some in the corporate sector. I know of constituents who regularly go without medicine because they can't afford it. I know of constituents who have dropped or reduced insurance on their property, their belongings or their car or their health insurance because they can't afford it. People are dropping Support at Home services which they're entitled to because they can't afford their contribution. 

More broadly, we see deteriorating public services—for example, the cuts to face-to-face supports like what used to be provided by the Australian Taxation Office. There was robodebt, which really affected the confidence that many in the community had in the government and the bureaucracy. We're still seeing, despite claims to the contrary by the government, the declining value and availability of Medicare, the outsourcing to contractors and consultants, taxes going up, increased means testing and reduced universal service provision. In Tasmania, again, the public health system is stretched to breaking point. Public transport in Tasmania, including Hobart, is flimsy at best. No wonder people are unhappy, because all of these issues are piling on top of each other. 

There have been the questions over integrity, which help to explain the declining trust in government. Just in recent weeks, we've had the revelations that the so-called Major and Local Community Infrastructure Program, which was worth about $560 million, was invite only, and three-quarters of the $560 million went to Labor seats. I accept the explanation by the Prime Minister that it's delivering on election promises, but still we see three-quarters of an invite only program going to Labor seats. No wonder people are cynical. No wonder there is something bordering on a collapse of trust in what I'll call the political class. 

Then there are the misguided priorities: insufficient increases from the government to income support, insufficient family and domestic violence funding, no effective tax on gas exports, and no effective gambling reform, despite all the hoopla several weeks ago—all while the community is witnessing billions spent on fossil fuel subsidies, nuclear submarines and so on.. Just yesterday, we had in the parliament a two very telling issues: the passage through the lower house of the legislation for the $5,000 cap on allied health services for veterans, and then the debate about the cut to the private health insurance rebate for Australians over 65. These are people with no financial room to move. No wonder they're unhappy. They have a grievance alright. 

I could go on. I think we all understand, as well as or better than I do, why people are unhappy. Some are angry. Politicians are not in favour. Political parties are not in favour. People are looking around for some way through this—some alternative. I think it's reflected in the polling—not that some of the people or parties in the polls offer any solutions, other than that they're just not us. I think that is part of the appeal of some of these other parties and candidates. 

But, of course, every problem has a solution. If the political class recognises there's a problem and has the vision to solve it and to get cracking, we as a country can certainly afford to solve these problems that I described. We can reinvigorate public services and supports. We can strengthen integrity institutions and the guardrails. For example, we can give the National Anti-Corruption Commission more teeth, let them have public hearings, shine a light, and bring into the spotlight or into the daylight the problems that people are concerned about. We can reform whistleblower protections and FOI and secrecy provisions; improve ministerial and parliamentary standards; undertake more political donation reform and more electoral reform; have a more independent and transparent government grants program; and undertake lobbying reform. 

But do you know what we need to do more than anything else? We—everyone in this place, myself included: all the political parties, all the crossbenchers and all the senators—need to remember our job is to work in the public interest every minute of every day and to put aside our political self-interest or the political self-interest of our party, if we're in a party. I reckon that's the way ahead here. If the public know we are working in their interests at all times and we're giving it our best shot, they'll be more forgiving when we stuff it up. But the way things are at the moment, they think we work in a bubble. They think we work only for ourselves and that we're completely out of touch with them. You know what? To some degree, they're right. And they'll get it right at the next election when they pass judgement on all of us. We'll see how they go with their interest in other emerging politicians and political parties. 

Whitlam Electorate: Tourism 

Ms BERRY (Whitlam) (12:40): I rise today to talk about the importance of the visitor economy across my electorate of Whitlam. This is particularly important for the Southern Highlands part of my electorate. I would like to thank the tourism and hospitality businesses, producers and community leaders who make the Southern Highlands region one of Australia's most distinctive destinations. The Southern Highlands has always offered something special. It sits within easy reach of Sydney, Canberra and the Illawarra and offers visitors something unique: authentic regional experiences, beautiful landscapes, thriving villages with historical charm, world class food and wine, a renowned equine sector and a strong sense of community. 

I want to talk briefly about why the future of Australia's visitor economy is increasingly a regional story and why the Southern Highlands is uniquely placed to benefit. Australia's visitor economy continues to show its appeal and resilience. The government's THRIVE 2030 strategy is in place to help grow visitor expenditure to $230 billion by 2030, with some $95 billion to be spent in regional Australia. Last year, Australia's visitor economy continued to show strong momentum. Total visitor spending reached over $190 billion, up 6.5 per cent from 2024. This was driven by solid domestic travel activity and, encouragingly, a rising international spend. 

While domestic overnight trips remained relatively stable at 113 million, domestic day trips grew strongly and were a major contributor to tourism in 2025. This highlights the ongoing desire of Australians to travel, with a preference for lower cost and shorter length travel options at the current time. Many Australians continue to travel to regional Australia, where nearly half of the domestic overnight spend occurred in 2025. In addition, international arrivals increased to nearly nine million people in 2025. There was strong growth in arrivals from many international markets, with particularly strong growth from the United Kingdom and China. International visitors to Australia last year typically stayed for longer and spent more than in previous years. This resulted in a record $39 billion international visitor spend in Australia in 2025. 

Travel to Australia for holiday purposes, major events, education and to visit friends or relatives all contributed to growth. On the supply side, industry capability also grew. Accommodation room numbers, flight route connectivity, tourism employment and tourism business numbers all increased last year. Investment in tourism infrastructure also grew, signalling confidence to invest in the sector's long-term growth. Key milestones facilitating growth include the opening of the new Western Sydney International Airport, increased aviation capacity through key ports, specifically from the Asia-Pacific, and investment in hotels and accommodation. Forecasts point to a positive outlook in growth of international arrivals and visitor spend over the period to 2030. 

Importantly, regional tourism has outperformed expectations. That matters for communities like the Southern Highlands because regional tourism is so important for our local economy. Our visitor economy supports local jobs, creates new businesses, drives investment and keeps young people building their careers here in our local community. This year in the Southern Highlands, annual visitor numbers are expected to be close to two million, which is an extraordinary number. To put this in perspective, this is the same number of people that visit Byron Bay each year. 

Australians are increasingly choosing shorter trips, destinations closer to home and experiences that offer value and quality. That trend is particularly encouraging for the Southern Highlands. The proximity of the Southern Highlands to Sydney, Canberra and the New South Wales South Coast places this part of my electorate in the sweet spot: close enough for day visitors, yet diverse enough to encourage overnight stays and extended weekend escapes. With the new airport in Western Sydney, the Southern Highlands is well positioned to receive more arrivals from this nearby major transit point. 

The Southern Highlands Destination Strategy identifies tourism, in addition to agribusiness and the equine industry, as priority growth sectors capable of creating jobs, attracting investment and strengthening regional prosperity. Visitors may arrive for business, family gatherings or an equestrian event, but they leave having also visited a cellar door or enjoyed a local brewery, purchased regional produce and spent time exploring local villages and attractions. That interconnected visitor journey is where the greatest economic value lies for regional economies like the Southern Highlands. Visitors increasingly want experiences, not transactions. The region's wineries are a great example. Visitors want to meet winemakers, see where the produce is grown, participate in tastings, attend seasonal festivals and understand the story behind the product. The farm, vineyard, brewery and paddock are increasingly part of the visitor experience itself. The wine industry delivers significant benefits across the Southern Highlands, strengthening tourism, supporting businesses and creating local jobs. It's an important part of what makes our region so attractive. 

The Southern Highlands has built a reputation that many regions would envy. We're recognised for producing elegant, cool climate wines with real character. Our altitude, our climate and our unique growing conditions give us a point of difference that can't be replicated elsewhere. The vineyards of the Southern Highlands benefit greatly from the region's cool climate, allowing production of unique wines that are appreciated across the country and across the world. I was pleased to attend a Southern Highlands wine industry roundtable at Bendooley Estate recently, where a range of stakeholders and local winemakers came together to discuss the future of this growing sector. It was incredibly valuable to hear firsthand some of the challenges being experienced by our local winemakers, as well as where the opportunities for this important part of our tourism sector may lie. Collaboration between businesses and stakeholders is an important factor, which will continue to ensure the ongoing success of our local visitor economy. 

Local businesses and stakeholders in the Southern Highlands place a high value on the local natural environment. Environmental protection is not simply a conservation issue; it's an economic issue. Visitors come to places like the Southern Highlands precisely because of our local natural environment. We also know that many travellers look for sustainability options. In Southern Highlands businesses, sustainability is already being embraced as a competitive advantage. This is part of building a destination reputation that stands out, and there are so many ecologically grounded examples here to mention, whether that's riding and bushwalking trails, wellness retreats and farm stays, or picking and tasting produce straight from its natural source. 

These sustainably minded offerings are also meeting a real interest in immersive experiences. The key challenges are scale, collaboration and innovation. These can be and are being met by building partnerships across sectors—for example, wineries working with accommodation providers, equine businesses partnering with restaurants and transport providers, and producers collaborating with event organisers and entrepreneurs packaging experiences that encourage visitors to stay an extra night and spend a little more overall by coordinating the message and knowledge about what each business offers. 

The visitor economy presents an enormous opportunity across my electorate. It's important to me to continue to support the growth of the visitor economy because it creates jobs, supports local enterprise, protects our environment and strengthens the unique character of our remarkable region. The Southern Highlands attracts visitors throughout the year with our beautiful countryside, historic villages, gardens, wineries, restaurants, galleries, markets and unique local experiences. Destinations such as Bowral, Berrima, Mittagong, Moss Vale and Robertson offer visitors a diverse range of attractions and help make the region a popular weekend and short break destination. Accommodation providers, cafes, restaurants, wineries, retail businesses, tour operators and local producers all share in the benefits. This supports employment and helps sustain small and family owned businesses. Importantly, tourism activity creates opportunities for young people to work and live in their local community. Tourism also strengthens the broader community by encouraging investment in local infrastructure, events and attractions. By valuing the Southern Highlands' natural beauty, heritage and food and wine culture, the region can continue to attract visitors, support local jobs and ensure tourism remains an important pillar of the Southern Highlands economy. 

