Mr VIOLI: Prime Minister Albanese's $5,000 annual cap for veterans who require allied health treatments shows just how out of touch Labor is, and risks veterans missing out on care and support. They put their own health and safety on the line to protect our country, and there is a clear reason the Department of Veterans' Affairs exists: to help and support our veterans when they return from service. Many return with complex trauma, chronic injuries and illnesses, and the proposed $5,000 annual cap does not stretch far enough for the treatment of these conditions. Our veterans have served our nation and they shouldn't have to beg for the care and support they need and deserve. Prime Minister Albanese, it's time to scrap the cap. 

In my community, reliable telecommunications are essential in keeping our community and region connected and safe, and, like so many residents across Australia, I know the heart stopping feeling of not having phone service when you need it most. I've witnessed the impact of communications going down during the 2009 Black Saturday bushfires and again during the June 2021 storms that ravaged my community and the Yarra Ranges. During the evening of that storm event, more than 10,000 requests were made for assistance, and after the first five hours the need for assistance didn't stop—it was residents' ability to call for help that did. The former Liberal government funded local mobile towers improving our local connectivity. But now Labor has cut the program and we risk going backwards. Under Labor, we're not getting new mobile towers in black spots where we need them most. We deserve better. We deserve to stay connected and have stronger communications all through our region. 

Superannuation 

Domestic, Family and Sexual Violence 

Ms BELYEA (Dunkley) (13:12): Over the past few weeks we've continued to hear from One Nation the proposition that raiding the retirement savings of working Australians is somehow a solution to the cost-of-living pressures people are facing today. It isn't, because the people who will pay the price are not the people sitting comfortably with multiple assets and financial advisers; they are the people I represent in Dunkley—nurses working double shifts, tradies whose body won't last forever, teachers helping shape the next generation, young families trying to keep up with rising bills, the renter wondering if they will ever own a home, and the single mum stretching every dollar just to get through the week. One Nation wants Australians to believe this is about choice, but, let's be honest: if someone is struggling to pay the rent, put food on the table or keep the lights on, that is not a real choice. We should never build public policy around asking people to sacrifice today just to survive tomorrow. 

Superannuation is not a government handout. It's not a political piggy bank. It is not money waiting to be raided whenever politicians run out of ideas. It is the hard-earned money of Australian workers, money set aside over decades so people can retire with dignity, independence and security. For many Australians, particularly renters, superannuation may be the only substantial asset they ever have—that makes it even more important to protect, not weaken. 

Under One Nation's proposal, workers could divert part of their compulsory superannuation contributions into their weekly pay packet. For many Australians, that amounts to around $44 a week. Let's put that into perspective: $44 a week might fill part of a fuel tank or a few bags of groceries or help cover a bill this week. But what is the cost? Experts have warned that workers could retire at least $25,000 worse off because of the loss of compound returns. The real cost doesn't arrive next Friday; the real cost arrives decades later when someone reaches retirement age and discovers the nest egg they spent their whole working life building isn't there. Here's what Australians deserve to know: it will leave Australians with less money in retirement and less security when they need it most, and then the very politicians encouraging Australians to raid their super today will be asking why people do not have enough savings tomorrow. 

I know what financial insecurity feels like, because I have lived it. I have spoken before in this chamber about my experience of domestic and family violence. For much of my 20s and 30s, life was marked by instability, trauma and uncertainty. Like many women, I worked hard to rebuild my life. My work was my safe place. During that time, one of the few things that gave me peace of mind was knowing that every pay packet was helping me build something for my future. My superannuation represented security, independence and hope. No-one could take away the fact that every week I was building a more secure future for myself. I know there are countless women across Australia, including in Dunkley, who feel exactly the same—women who have escaped violence, rebuilt their lives and know how important financial security is. 

That is why I cannot support any proposal by One Nation or the opposition that weakens the safety net for women, because we also know that financial abuse is one of the most common forms of domestic and family violence. Money becomes a weapon. Control becomes a weapon. Unfortunately, superannuation becomes a target too. We saw the dangers during the Morrison government's COVID-era early release scheme. Evidence provided to parliamentary inquiries following COVID showed women were pressured and coerced into accessing their superannuation by abusive partners. Money intended for their future ended up being used for gambling and to purchase drugs. So, when One Nation tells Australians that this is about freedom, my question is simple: whose freedom and at what cost? For many victims-survivors, easier access to super does not create freedom; it creates vulnerability and insecurity, and it perpetrates violence. 

As a government MP representing Dunkley, I say women deserve better, low-income workers deserve better and young Australians deserve better. That is why this government has taken a very different approach. We are supporting people experiencing domestic and family violence, introducing superannuation on paid parental leave and delivering payday super so workers receive what they have earned sooner, lifting wages. We are working really hard to tackle the causes of financial pressure. We are not telling Australians to withdraw their retirement savings and hope for the best, because real leadership is about solving problems, not shifting them, and shifting them is exactly what One Nation's proposal does. It takes today's problem and hands it over to the next generation of retirees. It is not responsible leadership or governance. It is irresponsible. 

For a young woman in Langwarrin, a tradie in Carrum, a nurse in Frankston or a family renting in Seaford, superannuation represents something much bigger than a balance on a statement; it represents security, independence and dignity in retirement. Most importantly, it represents hope for the future. That is what is at risk here. 

This debate is not really about choice. It is about whether we protect Australians' future or ask them to mortgage it. It is about whether we stand up for long-term financial security or chase short-term political headlines. I know where I stand. I stand with the people of Dunkley, with workers and with women. I stand with young Australians who are trying to get ahead. And I stand against any policy that leaves Australians poorer in retirement simply to create the illusion of relief today. Every Australian deserves dignity in retirement. Every Australian deserves financial security. And every Australian deserves leaders who are prepared to protect both. 

Barton Electorate: Infrastructure and Economy 

Ms AMBIHAIPAHAR (Barton) (13:20): The people of Barton have spoken, and they have three priorities: local infrastructure, the economy, and the cost of living. I'll use this grievance debate to outline these key priorities and the action residents need. The first is local infrastructure. In our dense Sydney community, people travel the same routes every day and repeatedly confront unsafe intersections, potholes, rat runs and truck hazards. Many of these issues fall to the state and local government, but I will keep advocating on them and ensure that we have a champion to make sure we have the funding support. 

The juncture of Bexley Road and William Street in Clemton Park and Earlwood is widely regarded as one of the area's most dangerous intersections. Turning onto Bexley Road means navigating blind spots on a very busy road, with residents repeatedly reporting near misses, including those involving buses and other vehicles. Nearby we have Clemton Park Public School, which brings a lot of school traffic, buses and young pedestrians together at peak times. The community has raised this for many years, yet it remains unresolved. We need an engineering review and serious investment now, not after a preventable injury. 

Forest Road and Stoney Creek Road are also among Barton's busiest arterial roads and corridors, and Bexley residents are paying the price. One survey respondent said the issue has divided the community, whilst others say the traffic is completely out of control. Congestion and unsafe conditions disrupt school drop-offs, endanger pedestrians and block access to local businesses. Excess traffic spills into the residential run-off, while high tolls leave motorists little choice but to stay on congested local roads. Successive state and council governments have failed to resolve this issue. As another resident said, the neighbourhood is 'drowning in trucks and pollution,' and residents deserve proper traffic modelling and meaningful investment. 

We need to prioritise safety around the Goodstart Early Learning centre in Carlton and South Hurstville. Vehicles speed past the Carlton centre near Planthurst Road and Park Road, whilst the South Hurstville centre on Woniora Road lacks adequate crossing support. Toddlers and preschoolers arrive and leave at the busiest times, and parents and educators face an obvious daily risk. The community has petitioned for action. Georges River Council must provide traffic support, particularly around calming, signage and safe crossing, before someone gets seriously hurt. I can't fix this overnight, but these are definitely on my list, and I will advocate for them at every opportunity. 

The second issue is the economy. Residents already face rising costs of fuel, housing and electricity, and the proposed data centre at the former Kogarah golf course has sharpened concerns. Overseas experience shows the strain that data centres can place on communities, and households rightly fear higher bills, while a private development consumes vast amounts of power and water. The concept plan was valued at up to $2.8 billion, including more than 25,000 square metres of data centres, warehouses, logistics facilities, hotels and offices. Buildings of up to 70 metres are proposed besides the Cooks River and residential neighbourhoods. This state-significant development raises serious concerns about scale, noise, height variations and about placing a power- and water-intensive industrial facility beside homes, including introducing pressure on the Cooks River. 

I do not want to say that we must block development full stop. After all, we live right next to Sydney airport. And I can certainly see the need for logistics and storage capacity as well as an increased number of homes so close to a major employer and service hub. After all, our Barton community has grown and evolved because of its close proximity to Sydney airport, but that doesn't mean that the future growth should not be well-considered, balanced and with residents at the heart of its planning. The community deserves genuine consultation, strong safeguards and transparency, not vague assurances and closed-door decisions. 

I welcome the federal government's mandatory national standards requiring data centres to add as much renewable energy to the grid as they consume and pay their share of network upgrades. Households must not subsidise private industry developments. I have raised concerns with our state member—Stockland and John Boyd Property—and will keep pushing to ensure that local communities do not wear the cost. Again, I want to reiterate that I understand the importance of data centres in our country. We must store Australian data on Australian soil. As Assistant Minister Charlton puts it: 

Imagine if our banks, our hospitals and our government systems all depended on computers sitting in another country. All our data, offshored to the lowest bidder. Most Australians would rightly ask: why are we trusting someone else with something so important? That's not independence. That's dependence. 

So you will not find me as a NIMBY naturally but, instead, I will always be championing for considered development that puts community first, development that makes our economy and society much stronger, and any proposals must be put to the people first rather than the developer's bottom line. 

Finally, there is the cost of living. I'm proud of the targeted relief we have delivered, including $25 medicines, higher bulk-billing rates across the community and greater support to help first homebuyers enter the housing market. But the closure of the Kingsgrove Post Office demonstrates one pivotal and deeply frustrating truth: too often, profit is still being put before the people. The closure proceeded despite a petition with more than 250 signatures and a united community campaign. Practical alternatives, including streamlined post services nearby, were not properly considered or pursued. 

Older residents and culturally and linguistically diverse communities have been hit particularly hard. They cannot simply be told to go online or travel further for a trusted local service, and Kingsgrove has already lost too much foot traffic, most of its banks and its hardware store. Removing the Post Office strips another essential service from the town centre and hurts local businesses. Worse, Australia Post failed to consult before finalising this decision. This is unacceptable. Residents must be consulted early and honestly before future cuts are considered. One resident put it perfectly when they said, 'Locally, I'm seeing shops that have been in Kingsgrove for over decades and survived COVID closing'—example: two greengrocers—'leaving much less choice for residents and forcing people to travel further away and spend more on the transportation to buy and do things that we used to be able to do locally.' The petition calls for no further Post Office closures, and that demand must be taken quite seriously. Communities are not line items to be cut wherever a balance sheet demands it. Essential services must put people first. 

Across all three priorities, Barton residents are asking for the same thing: to be heard, to be respected and to be put ahead of buck passing and private profit. They should not have to wait for a tragedy before dangerous roads are fixed, carry the costs of poorly planned development or fight to retain essential services. These are reasonable demands, and local and state governments and corporations must stop treating them as optional. I'll keep taking these concerns to every level of government, pressing for answers and demanding action until Barton communities receive the safety, the fairness and the respect they deserve. 

