CONSTITUENCY STATEMENTS 

Artificial Intelligence 

Ms SPENDER (Wentworth) (16:00): In the last week, we've seen leaders in the world's pioneer AI companies like Anthropic and OpenAI paint an alarming picture of the speed at which technology is developing, potentially beyond human controls. These CEOs spoke about their concerns and also their own responsibilities but they are calling on governments around the world to step up into this space, and I believe that this is urgent. I want to put on the record very strongly that I think that Australia needs to embrace AI. I recognise the benefits. I believe that Australian businesses need to seize the opportunities for productivity, and, if we don't, we will be left behind. I see the role for data centres in our country, both from a sovereign capability piece but also because of the potential benefits to the energy grid and the clean energy transition. 

But we also need to be honest about the risks and we need to be addressing those actively. I know that my community is concerned. I launched a survey earlier this week about AI and already have had hundreds of responses. An overwhelming number, 71 per cent, said they were very concerned about AI in general and listed catastrophic risks such as AI takeover, AI-enabled terrorism or extreme threats as their No. 1 concern, and other concerns included misinformation, disinformation and threats to democracy, environmental and climate impacts, job losses and economic disruption. The vast majority of respondents, 84 per cent, said that the government was not doing nearly enough to appropriately deal with the risks of AI. To be honest, I do agree with this. The government has been very slow to act. It took them until the end of last year to bring in an AI strategy, despite being in government for an entire term without one. Other governments have moved faster than them and this was slow. The government seems to be paying more attention now, and I welcome that, but it still has a long way to go. 

I want to talk about four things I think the government needs to step up on. Firstly, it needs to step up on AI safety. My understanding is that currently the AI Safety Institute, which the government established, has a budget of around $30 million over four years. People are putting the risk that AI destroys humanity at around 10 per cent. It doesn't seem like we are being serious about the potential impact of AI when we're managing the risks with so little money. 

Secondly, Australia needs to work with other middle powers on this. USA and China are leading the way, but we must work with other middle powers in terms of making sure that we have a role and a step and a control in this space. Finally, we need to actively capture the benefits much more strongly. That means not only helping Australian businesses but also setting ourselves challenges, like how AI can solve national problems and national concerns, some national missions that AI can deliver to really improve our society as well. 

Deakin Electorate: Vocational Skills and Training 

Mr GREGG (Deakin) (16:03): To mark Skills Week and TAFE Day, I had the opportunity to visit Swinburne's Croydon campus and spend some time with apprentices training there. Some were people I was meeting for the first time; others I've had the pleasure of meeting before and seeing progress through their training. We had some genuinely substantive conversations because completing an apprenticeship is a significant achievement. But we shouldn't pretend it's always an easy road. There are real challenges along the way. There are financial pressures and the challenge of balancing work, study and life. There are questions about the nature and continuity of on-the-job training and supervision. And there are challenges for employers too, particularly the small businesses that make an enormous commitment when they take on an apprentice. Those experiences deserve our attention. That's why the support we provide matters, from free TAFE to financial support for apprentices and employers, and to the broader work of making sure more people can start an apprenticeship and, importantly, make it all the way through with the skills they need in industry. 

But what struck me again at Croydon was the opportunity in front of these young people. A qualification as a sparky, plumber or carpenter can take you almost anywhere. The sky is the limit—big jobs, small jobs, around Australia and sometimes around the world. It's a genuinely exciting set of careers, and I can't wait to see what they're able to achieve. The qualification is just the beginning, not the destination. 

I see the same sense of possibility every time I visit Ringwood Training, which I have had the privilege of doing many times over the last few years. It is a really a remarkable institution, an arm of Ringwood Secondary College, one of our great public schools providing nationally recognised vocational training to school students, apprentices and people who've already left school. And there's real breadth in what they do. They train young people in automotive, from their first introduction to the trade through to apprenticeships in light vehicle mechanical technology. They teach engineering and fabrication, developing the welding, machining and metalworking skills that are needed across our manufacturing industry. And there's more that they can build on. There's great opportunity for them to expand to electric vehicles, as well. I'm keen to support them however I can in getting access to the cars and technology through government and through industry contacts, because we really do need to be readying these fantastic young people for the jobs of the future. Then there's MEGT, based in the centre of my electorate, doing another essential part of the job: connecting apprentices with employers and helping make those relationships work. 

Across all three, what I've come away with is an enormous sense of pride and gratitude: pride in the apprentices who are backing themselves, learning a skill and building the future; gratitude to the teachers, trainers and employers who invest their time and experience in the next generation; and gratitude to the apprentices and employers themselves who speak candidly with me about their experience and about what is working, what isn't working and what we still need to do better. Those conversations matter. Our job in government is to listen to them, to keep improving the system around them and to make sure, when someone chooses an apprenticeship, that they have every opportunity not just to start but to finish it and build something remarkable from it. To every apprentice I met: thank you for your time, your candour and your commitment to your trade. You should be proud of what you're doing. I certainly am. 

Senior Australians 

Mr FARLEY (Farrer) (16:06): I rise to speak for the people who built this country and who this government has chosen to ignore. Farrer is a vast expanse of rural and regional Australia. Pooncarie to Albury is a seven-hour drive. Distance is a daily reality, challenging access for our older residents to health care, services and support. Almost 20 per cent of my electorate is aged over 65, and around 19,500 rely on the age pension. The maximum single rate for the pension is $1,237.70 per fortnight; for couples, it is $1,866 per fortnight combined. That is what our pensioners are attempting to live on. 

Let me tell the House what survival costs. On modest figures, a pensioner household faces roughly $5,250 in rent increases, $3,250 in food, $875 in fuel, $450 in council rates and then essential services of health—a real annual burden increase of around $10,075, or 22.5 per cent of their pension, absorbed within one government's term. The pensions have not risen by 22.5 per cent. Something must give, and it is wellbeing, mental health and dignity. 

Then, of course, comes health. This government's private health insurance rebate changes will directly affect 14,000 senior residents in Farrer. Our seniors rely on private cover far more than their metropolitan counterparts, because our public infrastructure is thin on the ground. Policyholders are going to be affected by circa $255 per year. Health advocates have warned that seniors will drop or downgrade cover, and that burden will fall onto Albury Wodonga Health or Griffith Base Hospital, networks that are already stretched to their limits because Canberra could not see beyond the Sydney basin when it drew up this policy. 

Farrer is also home to 3,965 veterans, of which 2,576 claim support, as well as 3,100 NDIS participants and 4,200 people on disability pensions. Constituents who have served this nation, raised its children, worked its farms and paid their taxes are now asked to absorb these costs with no meaningful relief in sight. Every aged constituent in Farrer has a family beneath them—children, grandchildren and great grandchildren who admire and love them. When you ill-treat our elders, you do not fail just one— (Time expired) 

