Australian Labor Party 

Mr HILL (Bruce—Assistant Minister for Citizenship, Customs and Multicultural Affairs and Assistant Minister for International Education) (16:09): Dandenong in my electorate is a jobs-rich area, but it structurally has a higher unemployment rate than the metropolitan average. This was why I was so delighted to launch the Dandenong employment hub along with the assistant minister for employment—$1.5 million over three years. It's a place where anyone in the community can go to get help and advice. It's based at the Dandenong Council, but it's a partnership between all three levels of government. 

There is no wrong door. You don't have to be on Centrelink. You can be newly arrived, you can have been here for decades, you can be a mum trying to come back into the workforce or you can be the partner of a skilled migrant—you can go and get help and advice. It's about matching supply and demand, so employers are part of the hub. I encourage anyone who is looking for work to visit the hub. 

This was an election commitment that I made during the election, along with the member for Isaacs. It's an interesting thing, living in a democracy, isn't it? Candidates say, 'If I'm elected, we'll do A, B and C.' Then, if you win the election, you're supposed to deliver on the promise. I'm proud to be part of a government that is delivering on the promises we made at the election right across the country. I didn't think that was a controversial thing. But we now have the opposition and other parts of the parliament pretending it's rorting. They're saying it's corruption, that it's misusing grant programs. 

Well, I've got a different view. Despite the stupid media stories, which might get them a headline and deal with the sanctimonious schtick that some around here want to run, I'd just remind the parliament that the Auditor-General said—and I chaired the audit committee for years, and I thought this was bipartisan—that there are two ways to deliver grants. First, there are competitive programs, put out to everyone who is eligible. They were the ones the coalition rorted—coloured spreadsheets—and ministers had to resign. We have been four years in government, and every competitive grant program under our government has been administered with integrity. 

The other way, of course, is closed grant programs—invitation only—urgent, ad hoc, where executive government decides to do something. Or, properly, that's how you implement election commitments. That's what the guidelines say. That's what the law says. That's what the Auditor-General says. Everyone has to apply. They have a value-for-money assessment, done independently by the Public Service, and advice is put to ministers. If it's not value for money, ministers don't fund it. 

But don't believe me. Those proposing to do away with election commitments—I quote Senator Canavan: 'This is nuts, what you're proposing. You're effectively advocating now for laws to restrict candidates in what they can say and promise on getting elected. At the same time, you're saying you want to defend democracy. You're not proposing democracy. You're proposing technocracy. You're proposing that technocrats in Canberra get all the power to decide what happens in this country.' 

And I agree. When you're out there talking to people in the community, they expect you to deliver the promises you made. My community doesn't get all the billions of dollars of regional grant programs. We have poor groups that are not very good at writing grant applications, and now they actually got something. (Time expired) 

Veterans: Health Care 

Ms LE (Fowler) (16:12): Veterans have come to see me, and like many members in this House I have sat with them and listened. They were not asking for sympathy. They were asking for understanding. They wanted us, the people in this House, to understand what it's like to come home from a tour of duty and keep carrying it—the injuries, the trauma, the toll on their families. As a refugee from the Vietnam War, I know that Australians who serve gave. I do not take it lightly. 

In recent weeks we have heard a lot about the government's plan to introduce a $5,000 annual threshold for veterans' allied health care. Veterans organisations, families, clinicians and members of this House have raised serious concerns. My office in Fowler has heard directly from veterans who are worried about what this means for their care. There are more than 1,800 DVA clients in my electorate—veterans, service pensioners, partners and widows. Most rely on allied health to manage the lasting effects of service, stay mobile and live independently. 

The government says this is a review threshold, not a cap. It says care will continue while DVA makes a decision. If that is the promise, then guarantee it. Set clear time frames. Give veterans a right of review, because uncertainty changes behaviour. Will a veteran delay an appointment, put off seeing their psychologist or stop physiotherapy because they're worried about reaching the threshold? For veterans with complex needs, $5,000 can be reached quickly. That doesn't mean their care is inappropriate. It means their needs are complex. A dollar amount cannot measure clinical need. It cannot measure pain, trauma, mobility or whether someone is at risk of losing their independence. 

My office has heard from a veteran in Liverpool who is falling at home. His wife has had to call an ambulance many times because she cannot safely lift him from the floor. His occupational therapist supports a lifting device that would keep him safe and prevent him from more call outs. But he is worried about delay. He's worried about uncertainty. Support that keeps someone safe at home should never be harder to get than an ambulance after something has gone wrong. 

There must be scrutiny of inappropriate billing and overservicing. But the answer is targeted oversight of providers, not a blunt threshold that risks interrupting care for veterans who have genuine needs. Veterans and their families served Australia. They were prepared to protect our safety, our security and our way of life often at risk to their own lives. We cannot ask them to carry this burden as well. 

Superannuation 

Mr HOLZBERGER (Forde) (16:14): Paul Keating was both the architect of compulsory superannuation but also the first person from whom I'd ever heard the line, 'Good policy is good politics.' The corollary of that is that bad policy is bad politics. That's why I can't believe that we're going to be able to go to the next election with the future of compulsory superannuation on the table and at stake, because there is no better policy than compulsory superannuation. 

There's one thing which I haven't quite had the time, in the 2½ minutes I've got left, or in any of the speeches that I've given so far, to really talk about—one of the reasons that just really motivated me to get involved in politics in my thirties. In my young years, I was a young member of the Labor Party, and I thought that we could achieve change through government law, through collective action. Then, in my late twenties, I was working on sheep and cattle stations, and I guess I learned a different type of value—that sort of personal responsibility and taking care of yourself. So it was that I was working in a servo in Tenterfield and my then partner gave me a book on self-improvement. It was From Zero to $5 million in Five Years. I thought, 'What a heap of rubbish,' but by the time I'd finished it, I was hooked—not on the idea of money but on the idea that taking responsibility for yourself, taking responsibility for your finances, would give you that financial freedom to be able to follow your passion and follow your dreams. 

So I get this right-wing argument about how it should be all personal responsibility. I do get it. I do get where they're coming from, but the reality is that it just doesn't happen. There is a concept in that: pay yourself first. Pay yourself 10 per cent before you pay your taxes or any of your bills. Pay yourself first. But the reality is that that just does not happen. That's why compulsory superannuation is exactly that across the whole community. It is 12 per cent put away before tax and before bills, so that, at the end of the day, people can have an early retirement. They can retire at 60 in dignity. They can retire at 60 young rather than having to work through to the age of 67. 

The other thing that I don't get about this right-wing argument that has just gotten too extreme is that it actually takes pressure off the government. It is about people taking responsibility, looking after themselves, being able to retire without getting onto the pension. Without compulsory superannuation, there would be something like 60 per cent on the pension compared to 50 per cent on the pension. 

This next election is going to be very much about compulsory superannuation. It is a debate that I am looking forward to having because it does return me to that idea that it is about an early retirement, about people being able to take that time in the rest of their life to follow their passions and the things that they've been put on Earth to work for, not to be caught in debt and caught in a cycle of hard work. 

Himalayas: Floods 

Mr HAWKE (Mitchell) (16:18): I rise to join the chorus of parliamentarians in expressing our compassion to the people of Nepal for the recent disaster the Prime Minister spoke about in the House of Representatives. I want to thank members of the community in Western Sydney, so many of whom have reached out to me—people I've known over many years and excellent migrants in this country—who had family members in the region, both on the Nepali side of the border and in China and in parts of Tibet when that wall of water just swept whole populations and their dwellings away in those shocking scenes that we saw on our television screens across the world. 

I do want to thank the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade and the government for the response that Australia has provided. It's very appropriate. We have a great relationship with the country of Nepal. We have beautiful people who've come here and made a great home in Australia. Those are wonderful Australians who have family and people who were there visiting family, and they are to be given every assistance that we can provide them. I do want to thank in particular the actual officers on the ground, the disaster and recovery team that was sent to Nepal in two groups, searching for people in very difficult circumstances, working with authorities that are overloaded, trying to relay messages back home in a timely way, especially when you've got relatives on the phone, as you do as members of parliament. I thank my staff, who've worked so hard with the actual geolocation information that they think they have of missing relatives—very, very difficult content. I know everyone's been working around the clock in my office, in the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade, in the disaster and recovery team, in the government, in the foreign ministry and also the Foreign Minister, who are doing everything we can to provide assistance in what is a wide-scale tragedy. I ask the Australian government to continue its excellent efforts in lobbying. We've sought access to parts of Tibet and things that have not been available to the government of Nepal from China, and our good relationship there would help us ensure that we can do our best for people who are still missing and their family members here. I know we're all trying in this parliament to work in a very difficult environment among people's sovereign borders, but we urge everybody to have that common humanity to allow search and rescue to just get on with the job that we all want to do, which is to find people and find out what happened to people. 

I thank the Nepali community of Western Sydney—beautiful people, great migrants. To anybody online who has said anything negative about these migrants to this country—and there has been an unfortunate group of people who have—these are outstanding Australians. They are very welcome communities. They are part of the fabric of this country, and those comments are completely out of place. To the people of Nepal, Australia stands with you. We stand with the Australian-Nepali community as well in this time of crisis, and I thank the government for the work they've been doing. 

Barbagallo, Mrs Gayle 

Mr HUSIC (Chifley) (16:21): You think you know someone, and you always discover a surprise. I knew Gayle Barbagallo for decades, and it was only recently that I discovered that one of her favourite animals was an elephant. We associate long memories with elephants—that's all well known—but an attribute that also stood out for Gayle was that they looked after the group, they thought as a group, and they looked after the vulnerable. Long memories, think as a group, look after those in need—these were attributes that Gayle displayed quite often. 

We recently farewelled Gayle where I learned about her love of elephants and we were reminded about these attributes, and we were reminded about how much she loved our community. She didn't seek recognition for all her hard work; she just got on with helping people. She was a proud, longstanding member of our Labor family and served as the President of the Chifley Federal Electorate Council for many years. She also served on the state electorate council, particularly with Richard Amery, the former state member. She demonstrated this thoughtful, compassionate leadership that was grounded with a genuine desire to make our community stronger. She devoted decades to community service. She facilitated the St Mary's creative writers group, and created a welcoming space where people found confidence and friendship and their own voice. She chaired the Western Sydney Community Legal Centre, ensuring people doing it tough would have access to justice and support at times they needed. Through the Rotary Club of Mt Druitt, she just rolled up her sleeves and helped out whenever the community needed her. 

One of her greatest legacies was her decade of volunteering at Mount Druitt Hospital—whether through fundraising, supporting patients or helping visitors feel less alone, she always found ways to bring comfort to people. That extraordinary commitment to serving others was recognised when she received the 2018 Coral McLean Award, an honour that reflected exactly who she was. Beyond the titles and achievements, Gayle was a devoted wife, a loving mum, a cherished nana—a great nana—and, as mentioned in the beautiful tribute written by her granddaughter Sophia, it wasn't awards or committees that stood out; it was the Mondays spent drawing together, baking at Easter, laughing on the backyard swing and the simple joy of making a child feel endlessly loved. 

In an overflowing church, countless people came to say goodbye, and it spoke volumes about the impact Gayle had. I was incredibly fortunate to have known and worked alongside Gayle Barbagallo for so many years. Thank you for everything you did, Gayle. Please rest in peace and know we will always honour your legacy as generously as you cared for us and others. 

