Tara Wind Farm 

Mr LITTLEPROUD (Maranoa) (16:35): I'd like to bring to the parliament's attention a dangerous situation unfolding out in my electorate, in Tara, as we speak. It's been predicated by Vestas, a Danish renewable company that is looking to do a renewable development west of Tara, about 350 kilometres west of Brisbane. In seeking a development application, they put up testing towers to be able to understand the wind flow through there; I don't begrudge them that. But this application, which they put in place some months ago and which was approved, was predicated on it being placed in a certain position and with certain safety features put around it. 

We have already seen, as a result of the negligence of this Danish company, near misses by local aviators. In fact, for their application to the local Western Downs Regional Council, Vestas actually went and got an aviation consultant to give them advice, and that consultant failed to even understand that there were general aviators and agricultural aviators that flew in that part of the world. In fact, probably all they did was go and consult with Qantas and Rex, who fly at 30,000 feet. But these testing towers are effectively around 300 feet. So there's no way in the world that Qantas, Virgin or Rex would have any issue with this whatsoever. The tower was not properly lit, and these towers have wires that come down from them. We've seen that local aviators and agricultural aviators, who are flying fixed-wing aircraft as well as helicopters, are flying around these things without being able to see them. They have not been properly marked—they have not been placed where they were meant to be placed—and they do not have proper safety lighting. 

I've written now to CASA and the ATSB after local aviators and local landholders, like Keiran Cook, have had the courage to stand up and to say that something needs to change and that their lives are being put at risk by greedy renewable companies from Denmark coming in and not giving a rat's about the lives of those people that live out there, the people that make a living and have made a living producing your food and fibre every day. 

The fact that these men and women in helicopters and fixed aircraft fly around these things and can't see them—they've had near misses and their lives could be taken—is an absolute atrocity. The fact that Vestas have been allowed to put these things up in that part of the world, knowing very well that agricultural aviation is at the centre of how we produce your food and fibre, and the fact that they have ignored approvals that were given to them—the approvals have been ignored in every sense—is something that ATSB and CASA need to act on. I have written to them and I expect the people of western Queensland to be protected in every way. I look forward to seeing what ATSB and CASA do and I look forward to the prosecution of Vestas for negligence in this matter. (Time expired) 

Brisbane Youth Policy Challenge 

Ms JARRETT (Brisbane) (16:39): There are 187,000 people living in the Brisbane electorate, and 19,000 of those are schoolchildren. In the coming decades, those 19,000, statistically, will finish school, start a career, buy a home and start a family. Some of them will become doctors, nurses, lawyers, tradies—maybe even politicians. But our youth aren't just statistics; they are the future of our nation. 

Earlier this year, I tasked my 18-year-old intern, Florence, with the challenge of improving how we in government can engage with young people in civics, and she had the creative idea to launch the inaugural Brisbane Youth Policy Challenge. That brought together 35 exceptional students from across the Brisbane electorate to develop a policy solution for a local issue that was important to them, and they used the same processes that we would use in this place. I heard from students from public schools and private schools about a range of issues varying from education and transport to mental health, homelessness and intergenerational inequality. But the one thing that was prevalent across all the policy briefs we received was that young people aren't short of ideas; what they're short of is the platforms to have those ideas heard. 

So we listened. We had our highly commended team from Kelvin Grove state high school, and they analysed the challenges of work experience for high school students. They recommended a reworked program designed to reduce the mental health strain placed on young people, particularly those facing systemic disadvantage. 

The joint winners, however, were exceptional young leaders from Kedron State High School and Queensland Academies Creative Industries, known locally as QACI. They blew us away with their innovation, their professionalism, their creativity and their pragmatism. Kedron State High School students Angus, Asha, Jack, Mia and Toby, accompanied by Ms Meg Worsley, proposed a reversal of the Morrison government's Job-ready Graduates program to reduce what they saw as socioeconomic barriers for students entering legal and business degrees. QACI students Abby, Holly, Jake, Lixi and Tilly, accompanied by Ms Corinne Jennings, sought to implement safe zones on public transport. They proposed that we need safe zones like the ones they have in Victoria, and they believe that that will increase not just civilian safety but schoolchildren's safety and, importantly, support the ever-growing uptake of public transport. 

I am passionate about giving young people a voice. Too often they're part of a conversation in an audience and we ask them to listen and read, and, by the time they turn 18, they have to vote. But, last month, 35 students came together, and those students proved that they belong in the conversation, because the future of Brisbane is not some distant concept; it is sitting today in the classrooms of those 19,000 students. 

Capricornia Electorate: Capricorn Helicopter Rescue Service 

Ms LANDRY (Capricornia—Chief Nationals Whip) (16:42): I rise to recognise one of Central Queensland's great institutions, the Capricorn Helicopter Rescue Service—better known as CapRescue—as it marks 30 years of saving lives. What began in 1996 with a vision to give people across our vast region rapid access to emergency care is now a sophisticated 24-hour rescue operation. On 18 February 1996, CapRescue flew its first mission. Today, CapRescue's helicopter, Rescue300, and its highly trained crew stand ready around the clock every day of the year, at no cost to patients. CapRescue serves more than 300,000 people across an area roughly the size of Germany, from St Lawrence to Alpha and Agnes Water and offshore from the Capricorn Coast. Its crews respond to crashes, medical emergencies and search and rescue operations, reaching people on highways, properties and islands and at sea. In a region where distance can mean the difference between life and death, that speed and that capability are vital. 

Behind every mission is a human story, including that of 15-year-old Ty. Ty crashed his motorbike on his family's property near Ogmore, suffering life-threatening head, chest and internal injuries. CapRescue flew him to Rockhampton Hospital for urgent treatment before he was transferred to the Queensland Children's Hospital. For Ty's family, the sound of the helicopter arriving meant expert care was finally within reach when every minute mattered. Ty later said, 'Without the chopper, I would have had a very low chance of getting to help in time.' After surgery and months of rehabilitation, Ty was back on his bike within 25 weeks. His family then raised money for the service that had saved him, turning gratitude into support for the next family in need. That is CapRescue: neighbours helping neighbours and a community looking after its own. Over three decades, it has become part of the fabric of Central Queensland. 

Keeping Rescue300 in the air is costly, and local businesses, community organisations, sponsors and ordinary Central Queenslanders all play their part. Every fundraiser, sponsorship and donation helps ensure that, when the next Ty needs help, Rescue300 can take to the sky. That generosity has sustained CapRescue for three decades and reflects the strength of the communities it serves. For 30 years, CapRescue has been there for Central Queenslanders on some of their worst days. To every pilot, doctor, paramedic, aircrew, staff member, volunteer, fundraiser, sponsor and supporter thank you. Central Queensland is incredibly proud of you, and may CapRescue continue to be there for our community for many years to come. 

