Ms WATSON-BROWN (Ryan) (12:32): Earlier this year Labor tried to slash veterans' services, rather than make big corporations pay their fair share of tax, which was a huge betrayal of those who have served our country. Labor introduced a $5,000 cap on allied health services for veterans, a policy that fundamentally ignored the recommendations of the Royal Commission into Defence and Veteran Suicide—the same royal commission that uncovered the crisis of mental and physical health issues for veterans. 

Allied health is an absolutely crucial part of veteran health and wellbeing. This cap would have introduced another layer of bureaucracy, delaying essential care and costing lives. I've heard from veterans directly that, for many, the cap would have meant choosing between psychological care and physiotherapy. But Australians said: 'No. That is not on. We're not going to allow you to do that to our veterans, not while one in four massive corporations pay zero tax.' I was proud to present a petition to the House on this issue, with over 5,000 signatures, started by Rebecca, a local veteran. Thank you, again, Rebecca. Thanks to the enormous efforts of the veteran community, backed by the Greens, we forced Labor to scrap the cap completely. Labor picked the wrong side on this fight, which is unsurprising really. We've seen their track record, now, of backing their big corporate mates while leaving the rest of us to fend for ourselves while the cost of everything goes up and up. But there is hope. It just doesn't have to be this way. This government backflip is a very strong reminder that, when we come together and we fight together for what's right, we can win better outcomes for each other. 

Earlier this month, we saw the launch of Thriving Kids, the government's new support program for children with autism. This program, instead of the NDIS, will serve children aged eight and under. The Greens were very wary of this move, as we were concerned that the government was quietly trying to slash essential disability services. The initial lack of detail caused significant stress and anxiety to many families, including many in my electorate. However, now that these changes are going ahead, I'm even more concerned about the Queensland LNP government's reaction. The federal Labor government shouldn't be gutting the NDIS in the first place, but the Queensland LNP is refusing to sign on to the Thriving Kids program intended to fill that really big gap. They're threatening to leave Queensland children in the lurch with absolutely no support at all, and what does that mean for Queensland children? 

The community is rightly alarmed. Disability advocacy organisations have asked the Queensland government for clarity, but they're being left in the dark. Fortunately, no children with autism will be taken off the NDIS immediately, but, with changes starting in just over a year and Thriving Kids already being launched in every other state and territory, Queensland families need certainty. The Greens strongly opposed the federal government's NDIS cuts when they were first proposed. We saw that the changes were not co-designed and did not protect the interests of people with disability. We warned that the changes could result in families being unable to access the care that they need and deserve. 

This was another example of the government cutting essential services for everyday Australians while letting big corporations avoid paying tax. That tells you everything about Labor's priorities and whose interests they really are serving. But, instead of working with the Greens, Labor chose to do a dirty deal with the coalition backed in by One Nation. They chose to gut the NDIS and look after their corporate mates instead. Meanwhile, the people who rely on these services are paying the price. I call on the Queensland government to end the uncertainty and commit to properly supporting children with autism, and shame on Labor for gutting the NDIS in the first place and putting everyday Australians through this whole ordeal. The Greens will continue to speak out for people with disabilities and their families. 

Christmas is coming up, and it should be a time of joy and celebration with family and friends, a time for relaxation and a taste of living the good life in summer. We know that things are getting tougher. The cost of living is smashing everyone, and the RBA just announced another interest rate hike, the fourth in the last year. Groceries, mortgages, the price of fuel—everything's getting more expensive, and real wages are just going backwards. People are copping it. They're copping it bad, and I know that for so many Christmas just doesn't feel as magical as it should. So to try and bring back some of that magic, for the past few years, my team and I have been running a program called Elizabeth's Elves. It's a genuinely beautiful community initiative that helps neighbours to support neighbours in these tough times. 

This year, Elizabeth's Elves are supporting the fantastic work of Communify and Picabeen, two wonderful community service institutions on the west side of Brisbane. In my electorate last year, with the help of the wonderful Ryan community, they were able to support over 200 households and 500 people. We're collecting toys, gifts, food, toiletries and other non-perishable items to donate to the Christmas market days. Labor might have forgotten how to support regular people doing it tough, but it's clear from the years of success in these Christmas markets and Elizabeth's Elves that our community hasn't forgotten how to look after each other. 

In this current race to the bottom on immigration, Labor has chosen to either forget or ignore the human cost, the big toll, of their cruel new policies. People are hurting. They're feeling the economic pain of decades of poor decision making. Regular Australians are struggling to pay the mortgage, afford groceries or pay the fuel bill. That pain is real, and it must be dealt with urgently. Demonising immigrants is not the answer. The cause of the problem is much deeper. The political class on both sides have been captured by corporate interests. For decades, they've delivered policies that put too much power in the hands of major corporations and too little power in the hands of democratic institutions and everyday people. Put simply, we have lost control of our economy to billionaires and big corporations. It's easy and lazy for the government to shift the blame, and migrants are an easy target. That's shameful. 

I want to be clear about what these changes actually look like practically for real people. Here are just three examples of people who've reached out to my office for support in recent weeks: a mother, who's lived in Australia for 15 years, who has been booted out of the country, away from her only living family, to a place that is not home; a young father who may be forced to return to a country he hasn't lived in for 20 years, leaving his young children behind; and a young mum, unable to travel due to birth complications, who is unable to see her mum for the support she so desperately needs. These people are not making our lives harder. I'll tell you who's actually making our lives harder. It's the big banks, greedy corporations, billionaires and major-party politicians who do their bidding. Don't let them divide us against each other. 

Tiagarra Aboriginal Cultural Centre 

Braddon Electorate: Boat Harbour 

Braddon Electorate: Acknowledgements 

Ms URQUHART (Braddon) (12:40): I rise today to celebrate some very special milestones in my electorate of Braddon, milestones that speak to culture, community inclusion and the extraordinary people who make regional Tasmania and my electorate such a wonderful place to live, work and learn. First, I want to recognise the 50th anniversary of the Tiagarra Aboriginal Cultural Centre and keeping place at Mersey Bluff in Devonport. Officially opened on 16 October 1976, Tiagarra is one of the oldest Aboriginal operated cultural centres and keeping places in Australia. For half a century, it has held, protected and shared culture. Tiagarra stands on a place of great cultural significance. The bluff contains Aboriginal sites and evidence of occupation and cultural activity extending back thousands of years. It is a place where history is not simply displayed; it is lived, it's taught, and it's carried forward. Through exhibitions, guided experiences, school visits, community events and time on country, Tiagarra gives people an opportunity to listen, to learn and to better understand the continuing culture of Tasmania's Palawa people. 

This anniversary belongs to all the Aboriginal people who have kept Tiagarra strong through changing times. I acknowledge the elders, cultural leaders, staff, volunteers and supporters, whose determination has sustained this precious place. I particularly recognise the work of Six Rivers Aboriginal Corporation, which operates Tiaggara and creates opportunities for cultural education, employment, connection and wellbeing. I also want to pay my huge respect to Dave Gough, who has led Tiagarra through remarkable growth and change—tough times but good times—and for his work connecting younger generations to culture and country. I am proud that the Albanese Labor government has supported Six Rivers with funding to help Tiagarra open full time, to strengthen cultural engagement and to build stronger outcomes for Aboriginal people right in north-west Tasmania. The centre's 50th anniversary is not only a celebration of what has been achieved; it is also a promise that this culture, knowledge and connection to country will be respected and supported for generations to come. 

I also want to celebrate the transformation of Boat Harbour, one of the most beautiful and best loved places on the north-west coast. With the support of the state and federal government and the local council, a new Boat Harbour Beach Surf Life Saving Club and community facility is now at the heart of a safer, more accessible and more welcoming foreshore precinct. This $12.5 million redevelopment is a great example of what can be achieved when all levels of government and the community work together. The Australian government contributed $4.5 million through the Priority Community Infrastructure Program, alongside $2.7 million from the Tasmanian government and around $5.3 million from the Waratah-Wynyard Council. The result is much more than a new building. The surf life saving club now has fit-for-purpose facilities that will support beach patrols, training and the development of the next generation of lifesavers. The precinct also includes upgraded public amenities, improved parking, better footpaths, accessible beach access, recreation areas and landscaping. These improvements will serve local families, community groups and visitors for decades, and, of course, the wonderful Seekers Bar & Cafe has relocated into the new facility. It's long been a part of the experience of visiting Boat Harbour Beach, a place to meet friends, enjoy good food and take in the remarkable view that is offered there. Its new home brings hospitality, surf lifesaving and community activity together under one roof, making the precinct an even better destination for locals and tourists. 

I also welcome the recent federal funding of over $5,500 for a new beach wheelchair at Boat Harbour. This was funded through the Accessible Australia Initiative. It may be a comparatively modest investment, but its impact will be extremely meaningful. It will help people with disability enjoy the beach and the water with family and friends and participate more fully in community life. Our beaches and public spaces should be places where everyone feels welcome. Accessibility is not an optional extra; it is central to good community infrastructure. Combined with the wider precinct upgrades, the new beach wheelchair will open one of our region's most natural treasures to more people. 

None of these achievements happened without determined local leadership. Today, I want to give a special shout out to the mayor of Waratah-Wynyard, Dr Mary Duniam, as she prepares to step down after 21 years of service on council, including four years as mayor. Mary's leadership has always been grounded in a genuine connection with local people. She listens carefully, understands the challenges facing families and communities and works hard to make sure that their voices are heard. Her advocacy for better services and opportunities, particularly for children and families, reflects the practical and compassionate way that she has led. 

Mary has also been a trailblazer. Elected to council in 2005, she became deputy mayor in 2014 and Waratah-Wynyard's first female mayor in 2022. Her contribution has extended well beyond the municipality through leadership in local government across Tasmania and nationally, as well as her work encouraging and mentoring women to stand for public office. I have greatly enjoyed working with Mary over the years. I valued her honesty, her sound judgement and determination to secure the best possible outcomes for the people that she represents. Just as importantly, I valued her friendship. Mary should be immensely proud of the legacy she leaves, and the Boat Harbour precinct is one very visible part of that legacy. I wish her every happiness for the next chapter of her life. 

I also acknowledge two other Braddon mayors who are not recontesting. Peter Freshney, Mayor of Latrobe Council, and Marcus Blackie, Mayor of King Island Council. Peter, is stepping aside after 15 years as mayor. Over that time, he has been a steady and persistent advocate for a growing municipality stretching, from Latrobe to Port Sorell and the surrounding communities. Regional councils face real pressures from roads, housing and child care to emergency management and sporting facilities, and Peter has consistently worked to keep these needs on the agenda. Marcus has served four years as mayor of King Island, bringing energy and resolve to one of Australia's most remote communities. Leading a very remote offshore island council brings challenges that mainland communities do not always see—freight and transport, workforce shortages, access to services, infrastructure costs and the constant need to ensure that decision-makers understand island life. Marcus has worked full-time in the role, championing the island and fighting for its future. 

Regional mayors are champions for communities and can, too often, miss out. They know that distance should never mean disadvantage. They understand that a delayed project, a missing service or an unreliable connection has a very different impact in a small town or on a remote island. They bring local knowledge to the table. They build partnerships and keep pushing, often long after the cameras have gone. I want to thank Mary, Peter and Marcus for their service, for their persistence and for their commitment to their communities. Public service asks a great deal not only of those who hold office but also of their families. Each of them leaves with the respect and gratitude of the people that they have represented. I want to pass on my thank you to them and their families. 

From Tiagarra's 50 years of culture and education to the new Boat Harbour surf club, cafe and beach wheelchair, we are reminded what can be achieved through pride, partnership and persistence. These are stories of people caring for culture, looking after one another and building stronger regional communities. That is the true character of Braddon, and it is certainly worth celebrating, in my view. 

