Mr THOMPSON (Herbert) (12:49): I want to tell the House about a bloke from Townsville. He's university educated. He's a respected community leader. He runs a local sporting organisation. He's married, raising a family and wanting to give something back to his country. He put his hand up to join the Australian Army Reserve. And do you know what stopped him? His beard—not a criminal record, not a lack of qualifications, not a failure to meet essential military requirements, but a beard. He was told that, unless he had a religious, cultural or other approved reason for keeping it, he couldn't join the Army with it. At a time when Australia needs to strengthen its Defence Force and grow its reserve capability, we have a willing Australian being turned away over facial hair. And here's the absurdity: the Royal Australian Navy allows beards. The Royal Australian Air Force allows beards. Both services maintain professional standards, discipline and safety while allowing neatly groomed facial hair. But apparently the Army can't. Same Defence Force, same commitment to service, but different rules. 

I'm a former soldier. I understand military discipline. I understand standards—whilst I didn't always meet them—and I'd be the last person in this place to suggest we compromise operational readiness. But this isn't about whether a beard makes someone capable of serving; it's about who gets permission to have one. Defence has rightly modernised many of its policies around personal presentation and identity. The Army accommodates different religious and cultural practices that demonstrate that a modern military can recognise individual circumstances while maintaining professional standards. So why is an otherwise suitable Australian who simply chose to wear a neatly maintained beard unable to receive the same consideration? This shouldn't be about religion or gender; it should be about whether someone has the character, capability and commitment to serve this country—and if they do, Defence should have a very good operational reason for turning them away. 

The government recently celebrated 8,100 Australians enlisting in the Defence Force over the past financial year. But we need more Australians serving. Defence is also talking about strengthening the reserve workforce to meet future strategic demands. So how does it make sense to tell Australians we need them to step forward and then put unnecessary barriers in the way when they do so? We're investing heavily in recruitment, we're promoting opportunities across Defence, yet a bloke from Townsville puts his hand up and gets knocked back over a beard. You couldn't make this up. 

Let's remember what reservists bring to this country. They're teachers, tradies, doctors, business owners and community leaders. They balance civilian careers with military service and stand ready to answer the call when Australia needs them. In a city like Townsville, with a proud military history, we understand their value. We should be rolling out the welcome mat for capable Australians willing to serve, not putting a razor at the recruiting desk and telling them that that's the price of admission. 

I'm calling on the Minister for Defence Personnel, Matt Keogh, to urgently review the Army's beard policy. The Army should explain why it maintains a general prohibition that the Navy and Air Force have already moved beyond. We need practical and consistent standards across the Australian Defence Force—standards that protect safety, maintain discipline but don't unnecessarily exclude good Australians who want to serve. A beard doesn't tell you whether a person has courage, doesn't tell you whether a person can lead, doesn't tell you whether a person will stand beside their mates when things get tough and certainly doesn't tell you how much a person loves this country. Australia needs more people prepared to put on the uniform. When a good Australian puts his hand up to serve, we should be asking what they can bring to the Defence Force, not whether they are prepared to shave their face to get through the front gate. Minister Keogh, you need to fix this and let this bloke serve his country. 

There's a question that the people of Townsville deserve to have answered. When did the Albanese Labor government know about the structural issues surrounding Reef HQ? When did it know, and why weren't the people of Townsville told? What's unfolding with Reef HQ is deeply concerning. For generations, Reef HQ has been a part of Townsville. It's where school children learned about the Great Barrier Reef, where families visited and where tourists experienced one of the world's great natural wonders. It is an educational institution, a tourism drawcard and an important part of our city's identity—and it's been closed for more than five years. There have been five years of delays, five years of changing plans and five years of promises. But we need to go through the facts. 

In 2021, the former coalition government committed $80.1 million to refurbish Reef HQ. The building had known issues, which is why the refurbishment was required. After the Labor government came to power, the refurbishment was abandoned in favour of a complete rebuild. The project changed, the timeline changed and the cost changed, but there was no money added to deliver it. In May 2024, Senate estimates confirmed that the Reef Authority had never received the additional funding that it required to undertake the rebuild it had announced. Then, come August 2024, the Townsville City Council faced a significant decision. The city had $100 million allocated, through the Townsville City Deal, for a concert hall. Instead, in August 2024, the council was given an ultimatum by this prime minister: you can have a concert hall or you can have Reef HQ. It's the first time a council has ever been made to make that decision. The correct answer is, 'We'll have both,' but the council was forced to vote, and they voted for Reef HQ. A week later the Prime Minister stood in Townsville and celebrated the announcement. The government told our community Reef HQ had been saved. It said the additional funding would provide certainty. 

But here's the question: at the time that decision was made, what did the Commonwealth know about the condition of the Ross Creek wall? Had there been engineering concerns identified? Had concerns been raised with the Reef Authority? Had the department briefed the minister, and, if there were warnings, why weren't they disclosed before the $100 million was redirected? Townsville deserves to know the truth. We know that there have been reports suggesting structural concerns involving the Ross Creek wall. The possibility of relocating Reef HQ is being discussed—but should not go ahead. The Queensland government and Townsville City Council have raised concerns, and the community is left wondering how a supposedly funded project has ended up back in uncertainty. 

I want to know who knew what and when. No vague assurances and not another press release but dates, documents, engineering advice—that's what we want. When was the Ross Creek wall first identified as a potential risk? When did the Reef Authority become aware? When was the Commonwealth informed? When was the responsible minister briefed? Why did he keep it from the Townsville community? Was there any information available before the Townsville City Council redirected the $100 million in August 2024? These are straightforward questions. 

If the wall issue only emerged afterwards, then the government should release the records that prove it. But, if the government knew there were issues beforehand, Townsville deserves to know why that information was not disclosed, because a funding announcement is not informed consent, and celebrating a redevelopment while withholding material would be an entirely different matter. I'm not prepared to assume that happened, but I'm not prepared to let the government avoid answering it either. We need to know when the specific risk was identified and what decisions followed. How much has been spent? How much remains? What happened to the $180 million committed to this project? Our tourism industry awaits this. Local business awaits this. Townsville deserves answers. We don't want to see any more photo ops; we want to see some follow-up. 

Climate Change 

Mr ZAPPIA (Makin) (12:59): We live in the most uncertain times that I can recall in my lifetime, from the life-changing impacts of a three-year global COVID pandemic to the war in Ukraine, the rise of China's global influence, the recent military attack on Iran and, now, the fear of AI. People are understandably fearful of what the future holds. Simultaneously, essential, basic needs like housing are out of reach for many new, aspiring homeowners and the national health system struggles with daily demands. Not surprisingly for most people in both Australia and throughout the world, daily survival has become a priority. 

A regrettable fallout of the current pressures is that the critical issue of climate change and global warming are being sidelined. Recent polling confirms that climate change is dropping down the list of voters' most important issues, with only five per cent nominating it as their most important issue compared to nearly 30 per cent in 2022. Even worse, in many parts of the world, climate change initiatives are being scaled back or put on ice, in turn adding to the pace and threats of climate change. Within the broader community, discourse about global warming and climate change has stalled. Yet scientists tell us that the risks have in no way diminished and the consequences can be devastating and irreversible. A recent UK national assessment highlighted the alarming level of biodiversity loss and ecosystem collapse and the dire consequences for humanity that flow from that. The fact remains that the Earth is getting hotter, and the dominant cause is the burning of fossil fuels. 

Whilst Australians generally welcome renewable energy initiatives, we simultaneously continue to export record amounts of coal and gas, with Australia now being the third-largest fossil-fuel exporter. However, renewable energy technology, whilst welcome and effective, is hardly meeting growing energy demand when, in reality, total carbon emissions should be trending downwards if threats of climate change are to be reversed. Annual global temperatures over the next five years are predicted to be between 1.3 degrees centigrade and 1.9 degrees centigrade above preindustrial levels according to the World Meteorological Organisation. The consequences include melting ice sheets, coral bleaching, rising sea levels, water scarcity, disappearing flora and fauna, more extreme weather events that occur more frequently, a substantial increase in human heat stress and many, many others. 

The devastating fires in recent times, which saw 150 million hectares burned globally in the first four months of 2026 alone, with more since across Europe and North America, and the terrifying and destructive water and landslide in Nepal are confronting realities of climate change, whilst the human and financial costs of those events add to global financial pressures and stresses. It is also predicted that, in the years ahead, hundreds of millions of people will become climate change refugees, adding to the global refugee problem that we currently face. Australia's near neighbours in the Pacific will be some of the first and worst affected, and their problems will become Australia's problems. In the meantime, here in Australia, the Liberals', the Nationals' and One Nation's members openly backtrack from efforts to reduce carbon emissions, invest in renewable energy or accept the work of our global climate scientists. They are doing their best to prolong fossil-fuel use and constantly talk down the transition to electric vehicles, the Albanese government's investment in batteries and Australia's participation in the COP31 climate change conference. 

Climate change is real and, whilst climate change deniers, backed by the fossil fuel companies, run their dishonest, self-serving campaigns, the hard decisions are being left to future generations. Not surprisingly, the younger generations have now become the most active advocates for climate change action. They are the ones who will inherit the failures of today's generations. Survey after survey confirms that younger people are not dismissive of climate change consequences and feel betrayed by the lack of global climate change actions. It is understandable that dealing with the immediate cost-of-living pressures would be a priority for governments; however, there is no reason why climate change responses should be put on hold, as they have been in so many places. Good government means dealing with both. Doing so need not be contradictory. 

The Iran war and its subsequent ongoing disruption to global oil supplies highlights Australia's enormous reliance and vulnerability when it comes to diesel, petrol and jet fuel. Transitioning to electric vehicles can save Australians on ever-increasing fuel costs while simultaneously reducing carbon emissions. Large-scale production of sustainable aviation fuels in Australia could reduce greenhouse gas emissions from our aviation sector, create jobs and increase our resilience to supply shocks from events overseas. There are opportunities for farmers to electrify farm equipment—such as irrigation pumps, which often account for a substantial amount of on-farm diesel use—which could then be run on solar and batteries. I'm pleased to say that we're actually seeing some of that transition, with the sale of electric vehicles now growing at a much faster pace than had been predicted only a couple of years ago. That is good because, again, it not only diminishes the amount of carbon that is produced and released into the air but accelerates the transition that we need to go through if we are to reverse the effects of climate change. 

For all the criticism that I often hear about what countries are doing about climate change, the one country that is actually still sticking to its plan and agenda and probably doing more than any other country when it comes to climate change adaptation is in fact China, a country that is quite often in the firing line of the critics. 

Climate change is real, and the effects are already with us. The consequences are real and they are devastating. Governments know that. But, in many cases, they are too timid to act. Our younger generations know that climate change is real, and they have every right not only to be concerned but to expect stronger action across the world. They don't want just words and reports and they don't want just one conference. They want meaningful action and long-term climate change adaptation policies, something we have been talking about ever since I came to this place in 2007. But I haven't seen enough real work being done. 

I stand with those young people in calling for stronger action globally on climate change and global warming. The longer we delay our response, the more destruction will occur and the more drastic and costly the response in the future will be. It is a great injustice to future generations to burden them with that responsibility. 

