Mr TEHAN (Wannon—Manager of Opposition Business) (13:08): I rise this afternoon to talk on a number of issues that are relevant to my electorate. The first is the two-year anniversary of the Casterton hailstorm. Two years ago, there was a devastating hailstorm that went through the town of Casterton, the likes of which hadn't been seen, I think, for about 50 or 60 years. It caused mass damage, especially to roofs. It flooded, broke glass and caused immense damage. The problem was that, in the aftermath, sadly, the Australian insurance industry let the community down. It took the community gelling together and rallying to help each other and support each other to finally lead to the type of action which should have happened immediately. Once we called the Australian insurance council in, and once all the issues and all the problems were pointed out, action was taken. But it did mean that, for too long, too many people were left without the immediate action that they deserved. I think the Insurance Council of Australia know and understand the issues and have tried to act to make sure that their response, especially in remote parts of regional and rural Australia, is much quicker now and to make sure that they are there for the long term. 

I want to raise the two-year anniversary because I want to thank the community—the volunteers, the community groups, local government and everyone—for the way they gelled together and helped. I also just want to put on notice that there are still a couple of issues which are outstanding, and my office and I will continue to follow those up to make sure that we get proper resolution for those last remaining cases because, two years on, everyone should have had their issues dealt with. We want to make sure that, in the aftermath of what was a devastating hailstorm, people will get the financial support they need from their relevant insurance company to make sure that they not only can rebuild their lives but will rebuild their lives in homes which are fit for purpose for the 21st century. 

Roads, roads, roads. I say this, again, to the Albanese Labor government and to the state Labor government: spring is here. Now is the time to be getting your workforce and your crews. Get your funding out and make sure that you fix Victoria's roads, in particular, roads in western Victoria. Whether it be the Western Highway, where we've seen some tragic incidents and deaths, or just the state roads right across western Victoria—we need to see them fixed. The fact that they've failed to do their job has become a hallmark, in particular, of the Victorian state Labor government. When we look back and history tells the story of why there was a change of government in Victoria—and I'm confident there will be—it will be because potholes, in the end, told the story of a failing state. I say to Jess Wilson and her team: congratulations for committing $5 billion not only to fixing the potholes but also to making sure that when they're fixed they're done properly so the roads are upgraded and we won't have the continued deterioration that we see at the moment. 

I say to the Albanese Labor government: it's time you got serious again about your road funding. Instead of cutting it, it's time you started investing more, in particular, in Victoria, because Victoria's roads are a disgrace. What they need to see from you is not changing funding formulas so you rip money out; it's changing funding formulas so you put money in. The coalition, at the state level, has committed $5 billion. You should be looking, once, hopefully, we get that change of government, to working with them so that you invest as well and we can get back to having the roads that we deserve. 

About an hour ago I met a wonderful young mum from my constituency, Trudy Marr. Trudy's up here, in Parliament House, fighting for her young son. Her young son had cancer at a very young age. Fortunately he survived that cancer, but he will face ongoing battles, as many young people who have cancer at a young age, during childhood, face because the medication and the treatment that's needed to cure them can have lasting legacies in terms of their facing battles to do with their health going forward. What Trudy's up here in Parliament House for, on behalf of all my constituents who are dealing with this and on behalf of constituents right across Australia, is to ask the government to allocate, from within a pot of money, $10 million to a childhood cancer organisation and, thereafter, $10 million from existing funding which has been allocated. I'm going to lift up the booklet I have here. It says: 'Surviving cancer is not enough. Think life goes back to normal when treatment ends?' It's from the Children's Cancer CoLab. 

I say to the parliament that this is something we can all come together on to make sure this money is allocated, because we will change the lives of so many young Australians who have had cancer at a young age and who—through proper coordination, especially of services and especially of services in regional and rural Australia—could face a much brighter life if we can find this funding. Trudy, you're a community warrior, and I know you're not going to give up the fight on behalf of your son and on behalf of many young people right across this nation. I commend you, and I support what you are seeking to do. 

Tomorrow will be headspace Day, when we come together and acknowledge the establishment of headspace and what headspace is achieving in our local communities. I've been very fortunate to be able to advocate for headspace in my electorate, in Colac, in Warrnambool, in Portland and in Hamilton. We have just achieved funding now for a satellite headspace in Ararat, which the community has been fighting for for a very, very long time. My hope is that that satellite service in Ararat will become a standalone service, and we're going to continue to fight for that, but we've taken an important first step in getting that satellite service for Ararat. 

Can I thank all those people who work in our headspace centres in regional and rural Australia, especially in western Victoria. Getting the workforce that we need in these headspace centres is critically important and remains an ongoing challenge in regional and rural Australia. We need to be supporting organisations like the One Red Tree Resource Centre which help provide the employment in headspace centres by training up people in the services that we need in order to run headspace centres efficiently. 

For National headspace Day, which will be tomorrow, I commend everyone who works for headspace right across this nation. I commend headspace for what they're doing in changing the lives of young people who are dealing with mental health issues. Hopefully we can continue to support the rollout of headspace centres, especially in regional and rural areas where we need these important services. 

Melbourne Electorate: Community Events 

Artificial Intelligence 

Ms WITTY (Melbourne) (13:18): When I gave my first speech in this place, I spoke about Melbourne as a city that pulses with live music, breathes a soul of theatre and has creativity running through it. I've had plenty of reasons since then to feel even more strongly about that. I have the enormous privilege of representing a part of Australia where you can walk a few streets and come across live music, theatre, comedy, galleries, record shops, games, fashion and people making things you probably wouldn't find anywhere else. Melbourne has built that reputation over generations. A lot of it has happened in small venues, rehearsal rooms and studios above pubs, behind shopfronts and in places where somebody was prepared to give an artist a go. 

I was reminded of that recently when I toured the Melbourne Fringe Festival. Fringe has been part of Melbourne for more than 40 years. This year's festival has more than 450 events, and its open-access model means an independent artist does not have to go through a selection committee or audition before registering a show. There is theatre, comedy, circus, cabaret, music, dance and visual arts. There are established artists and people putting something in front of an audience for the first time. For 20 days, that work takes over theatres, pubs, shopfronts and community halls across Melbourne. You can see people trying things out. Some of it's funny. Some of it's strange. Some of it is even challenging. That willingness to experiment has always been part of Melbourne's culture. 

Earlier this year I visited St Martins Youth Arts Centre in South Yarra. They have worked with children and young people since 1980, putting them in rehearsal rooms with professionals and treating them as artists in their own right. They help create the work themselves and develop scenes, movement, sound, characters and performance. I really enjoyed seeing how seriously they take young people's ideas and creativity. People including Ben Mendelsohn, Catherine McClements, Gina Riley and Jane Turner have come through St Martins. 

Another area where Melbourne exceeds is gaming, and I recently saw the launch of Melbourne International Games Week at ACMI in Federation Square. ACMI is Australia's only museum dedicated to screen culture, bringing film, television, video games and screen based art together in the middle of Melbourne and welcoming around one million visitors each year. It has also taken video games seriously as an art form for more than 20 years. It exhibits and collects games, supports developers and hosts Melbourne International Games Week, a 10-day celebration of Australian and international video game culture and industry. Digital games development recorded average annual growth of 13.1 per cent over the decade of 2024 to 2025, the strongest growth across Australia's culture and creative sector. The broader cultural and creative economy contributed $70.3 billion in 2024-25. 

Another organisation I've been proud to support is the Social Studio. The Social Studio has been part of Melbourne's scene since 2009, creating training, employment and creative opportunities for people from refugee and migrant backgrounds through fashion, design and the arts. Their school, manufacturing studio and retail store give emerging creatives somewhere to build skills and find meaningful opportunities. 

When talking about art, I often come back to music. Melbourne's music scene is one of the things I love most about representing this city. On almost any night, you can find someone playing somewhere. There are rooms packed with people and rooms where the audiences can practically lean on the stage. There are musicians playing their first proper gig and others who have spent their lives performing. Around them are sound engineers, bookers, promoters, producers, venue staff, good audience members like me and all the people who keep live music running. 

Those smaller venues have a practical place in Melbourne. Long before an artist can fill a festival stage, they need somewhere to play in front of a much smaller group of people, and hopefully that's more than one. They need someone willing to book them and audiences willing to take a chance. The Albanese Labor government knows the pressure the live music sector has been under. We established Revive Live to support Australian owned venues and festivals and give Australian artists more opportunities to perform, develop an audience, hone their craft and earn an income. 

Some of the Melbourne organisations and venues supported, including the Grace Darling Hotel, the Bodriggy Brewing Company, Yah Yahs and Punters Club alongside Ability Fest and Glo Glo in Fitzroy. Across Australia, Revive Live has provided $19.3 million to 221 live music businesses, including 100 venues and 121 festival activities. Our government has committed a further $12.5 million for 2026-27. Labor's cultural policy, Revive, also established Music Australia within Creative Australia to support and promote contemporary Australian music and help Australian artists develop audiences and markets. 

As incredible as Melbourne's arts and culture scene is, it is under pressure and threat from unregulated AI training. If you make music, write, illustrate, photograph, design or perform, your work is your intellectual property. Australian copyright law gives copyright owners economic rights over their material, while creators and performers also have moral rights including attribution and protection against false attribution. Generative AI has raised serious questions about what happens when copyright work is used to train AI systems. The Albanese Labor government has already made clear it supports artists and their work by ruling out a broad text and data mining exception in Australian copyright law. Parts of the technology sector had called for such an exception. It could have allowed AI developers to use Australian creatives' copyright work, for free and without permission, to train AI systems. The government has ruled that out and has been clear that it has no plan to weaken copyright protection because of AI. 

The government's Copyright and Artificial Intelligence Reference Group is bringing creators, industry and experts together to work through what comes next, including how copyright material is used as inputs for AI and questions around transparency. The government is considering different ways of addressing this emerging technology—for example, whether Australia should establish a paid collective licence framework for AI or continue with voluntary licence agreements. It is also examining whether copyright law needs greater clarity around AI generated material and whether a small-claims forum could make it cheaper for copyright owners to pursue lower-value infringements. These discussions are ongoing, and I am pleased to know that artists and creative workers are at the heart of these talks. 

Melbourne gives me the chance to see how broad our arts scene really is—independent performers at Fringe; young people making theatre at St Martins; designers at the Social Studio; game developers, musicians, technicians and the people keeping local venues alive. The mix has taken generations of people to build. I am incredibly proud to represent them and proud to be part of a government that backs Australian artists and wants the next generation to have the opportunity to make a life and a career from their creativity. 

