CONSTITUENCY STATEMENTS 

Tasmanian Times 

Mr WILKIE (Clark) (16:00): I have received a whistleblower disclosure from veteran Tasmanian investigative journalist Mr Geoffrey Swan, a 10-year contributor to Tasmanian Times and who was acting chief editor of the publication until January. My office has had several dealings with Mr Swan this year via emails, phone calls and in person. Tasmanian Times was founded in 2003. It holds about 64,000 articles and has been a reputable and trusted local publication for years. The documents provided to me by Mr Swan allege serious misconduct by the publication and its former owner and publisher, Mr Alan Whykes. 

Mr Swan claims that Tasmanian Times has been leveraging its reputation to quietly profit from the promotion of illegal offshore gambling. According to Mr Swan's disclosure, when conducting an audit of the Tasmanian Times database he found 1,894 posts which contained hidden black-hat search engine tactics. Black-hat SEOs are features like hidden links and invisible text that are used to rank a website by violating search engine guidelines. They do this by manipulating search engine algorithms to increase a site's rankings. In the case of Tasmanian Times, Mr Swan alleges that black-hat SEOs had been used to funnel readers towards unlicensed offshore casinos. The posts are invisible to ordinary readers but highly visible to Google, which trusts the masthead because of its decades of credible reporting. It is relevant here that the Interactive Gambling Act 2001 prohibits posting links to unlicensed offshore gambling services. Therefore, if Mr Swan's allegations are correct, both Tasmanian Times and Mr Whykes stand accused of breaching the Interactive Gambling Act as well as misleading readers. 

To date, Mr Swan has referred his allegations to the Australian Communications and Media Authority, the Australian Taxation Office, the Australian Federal Police and the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission. But, as is often the case with whistleblower matters I raise in this place, each organisation has so far failed to act. Accordingly, and as this matter falls across several portfolios, I call on the Prime Minister to now coordinate a whole-of-government response to Mr Swan's allegations. 

In closing, I would add that Mr Swan is a recipient of the 2026 Walkley Foundation's freelance journalism grant in relation to this matter. I seek leave to table the documents provided to me by Mr Swan. 

Leave not granted. 

Indian Ocean Territories 

Ms SCRYMGOUR (Lingiari) (16:03): I rise today to talk about Australia's most remote communities, the Indian Ocean Territories. The Indian Ocean Territories are an important strategic and cultural part of this country. They represent the very best of modern Australia—a strong and connected local community grounded in their diversity. These communities are resilient. They know how to work together and have deep respect for what it means to be Australian. But often their distance makes them feel overlooked, and government must do better for these territories. 

During my recent visit, I met with local leaders to discuss Qantas's baggage allowance changes. Without proper community consultation, Qantas has reneged on its commitment and changed the rules for Islanders. This means people who rely on air freight to access essential goods have had the rug pulled out from under them. Islanders are rightly angry and are frustrated. We all expect better from Australia's national airline. Qantas needs to rebuild its social licence. Islanders are mobilising against these changes and working to have their voices heard in the boardroom of Qantas, and I will do everything I can to represent these voices. 

A key challenge is economic transition. We have to, with the community, build a robust local economy that provides quality jobs and a future for people on the islands. Last week I met with innovative and resilient local businesses on Christmas Island. When you're 2,900 kilometres away from the mainland, you have to navigate hurdles that other businesses on the mainland never have to. From unreliable food deliveries, access to skilled labour and high costs, in the face of these challenges businesses are adapting, but they need our support. I met with the Christmas Island Tourism Association, who are doing a fantastic job at advocating for a tourism led future and supporting local operators. To build an economic future and a tourism industry, it must rely on secure access. They need Qantas to come to the table to support this. We also need to ensure maritime deliveries are regular and reliable. 

On Cocos Keeling Islands, the community is at the forefront of climate change. Erosion and rising sea levels are causing major challenges for services and the community. As Australia looks to discuss climate change on the global stage, we must look after our own people, who should be at the centre of this, and all of the communities, particularly Cocos Keeling, which has been fighting and at the forefront of climate change. I am making sure that our government listens and supports that community to deal with the rising sea levels and all of the coming dangers that are encroaching on the fragile systems on the atoll out at Cocos Island. 

Medical Research Future Fund 

Ms BOELE (Bradfield) (16:05): During my time in office, I've had the heartbreaking privilege of meeting with Bradfield constituents to hear their stories of devastating loss and life changing diagnoses—in particular, Georgina, who told me of the loss of her beautiful daughter Vanessa to sudden cardiac arrest aged just 24; Nadine and David, whose daughter Sasha was diagnosed with a rare, recessive genetic condition at the age of four; and Lucy, who lost her young daughter to neuroblastoma. 

These families inspired me with their hope, turning their personal devastation into purpose and action. They have formed and joined advocacy groups. They've rallied scientists, medical researchers and universities to their cause. They have promising, high quality research in development: the application of CAR T-cell therapy to solid tumours, personalised gene therapies and targeted cardiovascular research. What they desperately need is funding. Australia has a well-established medical research fund, the MRFF, established in 2015 with the goal of providing $1 billion annually to medical research investment. Yet, since the fund was fully capitalised in 2022, annual investment has stalled at $650 million. That's $350 million short of what could be an eye-watering sum being withheld every year from the Australian scientific and medical research community. 

This is not just frustrating to scientists and medical researchers. The Australian Academy of Science has warned that low grant success rates are contributing to researchers leaving active research careers and being driven overseas. It means high-quality medical research projects are not being funded and that the world is being denied potential life-saving advancements and the incredible expertise of Australian scientific talent. It means that we're not building out Australian scientific capability or downstream capabilities at a time when we should be strengthening our long-term productivity. But equally, behind every promising research proposal that's not funded, there's a real person whose life is not going to be saved or improved because of this decision and whose family is left asking why, as they desperately try to raise funds for expensive treatments overseas. 

The Future Fund board has determined that it would be financially sustainable for the MRFF to make the full $1 billion annually available for research grants. I acknowledge my colleague the member for Kooyong for her sustained advocacy in this space. For Georgina, this extra funding would mean honouring Vanessa's life through investing in the work of the Centenary Institute relating to sudden cardiac death. For Sasha, personalised gene therapies would allow her to regain her lost skills—to walk and talk again. For Lucy, it would give hope to the Australian children who relapse with neuroblastoma and otherwise face a devastating 10 per cent long-term survival rate. 

Banks Electorate: Community Events 

Mr SOON (Banks) (16:09): Over the October long weekend, I held the first Banks Community Cup Table Tennis Tournament, bringing together 64 participants and more than 50 spectators in Penshurst for some lighthearted social competition. Table tennis is more popular in the electorate of Banks than you might expect, especially amongst our diaspora communities. The tournament was a great success with father-son duo Garry and Stephen Kwong emerging victorious in an incredibly close final with second place finishers Rodney Forrester and Mark Lancaster. I'd like to register my thanks to everyone who helped make today possible, including the St George & Sutherland Shire Table Tennis Association and the Georges River Association for the incredible assistance. 

On 3 October, I had the pleasure of attending a spring luncheon in celebration of the 80th anniversary of the Padstow RSL subbranch alongside the state member for East Hills, Kylie Wilkinson. For eight decades, the Padstow subbranch has provided much-needed support and camaraderie for local veterans. During the celebrations, we also had the opportunity to dedicate a new memorial cabinet, which was made possible by a grant of $10,000 from the federal government as part of round 9 of the Stronger Communities Program. I'd like to express my thanks to the subbranch president, Trevor Young, and the club president, John Evans, for putting together such a great celebration for 80 years of service. 

Finally, my community is benefiting from the Labor government's decision to double the Roads to Recovery Program to $1 billion annually for the current five-year funding cycle. Across the suburbs of Padstow, Revesby, Panania and East Hills this year, there are five projects funded by the Roads to Recovery Program that are either complete or underway—the most in a single year since 2015, with funding totalling more than $1.2 million. These projects are not all the federal government is doing for local infrastructure in the electorate of Banks, with the Black Spot Program, funding for Marco Avenue in Panania, funding delivered for stage 1B of the Henry Lawson Drive upgrade in Milperra and funding to fulfil the commitment we made to the community in 2022 that would fix the Revesby Police Station intersection that has been a problem for decades in our community. This government is delivering for our community of Banks, fixing roads, fixing infrastructure and making sure our community has the infrastructure and the support that it needs. 

