The reason I'm mentioning this today is that, sadly, when the Albanese government came to power, they withdrew $109 million from the project, leaving just $41 million from the original commitment. It went to the Suburban Rail Loop. So it was diverted from unsealed roads in the hills to those ripping off and rorting the Big Build, as we call it—or the big rort, it should be called, when it came to the CFMEU. Now we've had even the Victorian premier, Ben Carroll, acknowledge that the money must be better spent and they're trying to find ways of saving money. First of all, to the Albanese government: you need to reinstate that funding. It's so bad that so many local residents were planning on having their roads upgraded and working with Cardinia council, and the money was taken away. Where did it go? As I say, to the Suburban Rail Loop—the biggest rort around. The money needs to be returned, and I call on the Albanese Labor government to return that money. Finally, I'll just say it was a matched election commitment when Bill Shorten was the leader, and, sadly, Labor broke their promise and reneged on it. 

