I reference that in relation to this bill because I think, when we talk about health, we do need to talk about and, moreover, act upon the inequities of our system. Associate Professor Fielding is absolutely 100 per cent correct. I think the member for Barton would agree, too, that we do have a very, very good health system, and we're very lucky. 

It doesn't matter which side of politics is in government—well, it does. I'd sooner be in government than be in opposition, trust me. But, as far as our health system is concerned, I know there's often at election time these fibs told about the fact that the coalition is selling off Medicare and all these other things to try to get a vote, which is, of course, not true. It worries me that the Prime Minister pulls out his green and gold Medicare card and says, 'This is all you will need.' It worries me that the urgent care clinics are not distributed fairly—and they are not—across Australia. It worries me that there is an inequity in health between country and city. We don't need that sort of divide. 

This bill will remove the cap of $5,000. It always worries me when we start talking about caps of $5,000, having had that allied health experience with the $5,000 cap with veterans, but, thankfully, the government has finally come to its senses and said that it will change that. I just wait to see the devil in the detail with that. Removing this cap from the national joint replacement registry levy act that can be imposed per medical device listed on the prescribed list of medical devices and human tissue products for a financial year—so it goes, with this change, the levy will be allocated in proportion. I heard the member for Barton speak to that. The levy will be allocated in proportion to the actual volume of devices supplied by medical device companies, creating a fairer and more equitable distribution of the levy. Hopefully, that stacks up and certainly is as it is intended to be. The Australian Orthopaedic Association collects information from all relevant hospitals in Australia to define, improve and maintain the quality of care of individuals receiving joint replacement surgery. 

I do worry as well, though, when we talk about relevant hospitals. Again, when we look at the health legislation and health policy before us at the moment, we have the situation where the private health insurance rebate that over 65 have relied upon is being taken away, and that is bad in itself, let alone the fact that hospitals are very worried that this will push their patients onto the public state hospital system. Our state public hospitals already have long, long queues for certainly such things as hip replacements, knee replacements and joint replacements, per se. There is going to be a huge dilemma that that insurance rebate situation for over 60 fives—it's a cliff, and we're about to go over it. The worry is that we will lose some private hospitals. We will actually have hospitals close. These are not my words. That is already the fear being expressed by many in the health sector. Just as bad as that—and we don't want to see any hospitals close—is the fact that it is already difficult enough for our state public hospital system to cope with the number of patients they have now. 

I am very, very lucky in Wagga Wagga. I have the Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, which is relatively new in the scheme of things, and we've also got the Calvary Riverina Hospital, which has been operating for many, many decades, and both do a fine job. They're all in the one Docker Street medical precinct, and long may they not only service the entire Murrumbidgee Valley, which encompasses, quite frankly, probably around a quarter of a million people, if you add up all the people who go to that hospital from not just Wagga Wagga, a population of 73,000 or so, but also the Murrumbidgee irrigation area centred on Griffith, a population of 32,000 or so, and everywhere in between but service elsewhere as well throughout the Riverina and south-west and southern New South Wales. Fortunately, there is a good relationship between the public hospital, Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, and the private hospital, Calvary hospital, and we have other day surgeries as well. They all perform hip replacements, knee replacements and joint replacements, and that is good. 

The Australian Orthopaedic Association is provided funding for components of the registry's administration through a Commonwealth grants agreement which is efficiently cost recovered through the imposition of a levy under the Private Health Insurance (National Joint Replacement Register Levy) Act of 2009. The levy is paid by the medical device industry. This is the situation with the particular bill before us. I appreciate that it has the support of Private Healthcare Australia, the Australian Orthopaedic Association and the Medical Technology Association of Australia, as you would expect it would. I would like to think that something as important as this which will have some profound impacts upon the health sector would have received the stakeholder engagement and the stakeholder support that in fact it needs. The Commonwealth grants agreements are something that the health minister controls and runs. It is an important process not just with the public system but with the states as well. 

